            Pennsylvania Calculator
          </a>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              What is the average settlement for a rear-end collision?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              Rear-end collisions are the most common type of car accident in the United States, and they typically cause whiplash and soft-tissue neck injuries. The average settlement for a straightforward rear-end collision with whiplash ranges from $10,000 to $25,000 for minor cases. If there are disc injuries, nerve symptoms, or the need for injections or surgery, settlements can range from $40,000 to $150,000 or more. Liability is almost always clear in rear-end collisions, which helps speed up the settlement process and supports higher multipliers.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              Are car accident settlements taxable?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              Generally, no. Under IRS Section 104, compensation received for physical injuries or physical sickness — including medical expense reimbursements and pain and suffering damages — is excluded from gross income and is not taxable. However, if any portion of your settlement represents punitive damages or compensation for emotional distress not connected to a physical injury, those portions may be taxable. Lost wage reimbursements can also be taxable in some cases. Consult a tax professional for advice specific to your situation.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              How much do lawyers take from car accident settlements?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              Personal injury attorneys typically work on a contingency fee basis — meaning they only get paid if you win. The standard contingency fee is 33% (one-third) of the settlement if the case settles before a lawsuit is filed. If a lawsuit is filed, the fee often increases to 40%. If the case goes all the way to trial, some attorneys charge 45%. In addition, litigation expenses (expert witnesses, filing fees, deposition costs) are usually deducted from the settlement before the attorney calculates their fee. Always ask for a written fee agreement before hiring an attorney.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              Does having pre-existing injuries reduce my settlement?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              Having a pre-existing condition does not automatically disqualify you from recovering. The "eggshell plaintiff" doctrine in most states holds that a negligent driver is responsible for any harm they cause, even if a pre-existing condition made you more vulnerable to injury. However, the insurer will argue that some of your pain and limitations were pre-existing rather than caused by the accident. You must clearly document how the accident aggravated or worsened your prior condition. Your doctor's records and expert testimony are essential in these cases.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              How do I know if a settlement offer is fair?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              A fair settlement fully compensates you for 100% of your economic losses (all medical bills plus future medical costs, all lost wages, all out-of-pocket expenses) plus a reasonable pain and suffering component based on your injury severity. Use our <a href="/" class="text-link hover:underline">free calculator</a> to estimate your claim's fair value, compare it to the offer, and use the difference as the basis for your counter-demand. If an insurer's offer doesn't cover your basic economic damages, it is almost certainly too low. Learn how settlements are calculated in our guide on <a href="/blog/how-car-accident-settlements-are-calculated/" class="text-link hover:underline">how car accident settlements are calculated</a>.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <p>
          Understanding average settlement amounts is a useful starting point, but the only number that truly matters is the one specific to your injuries, your state, and your circumstances. Use the information in this guide as context, use our calculator to personalize the estimate, and do not let an insurance company convince you that a low offer is all you deserve.
        </p>

